  {
    "name": "2021-Mallorca",
    "date": ["2021-09-11", "2021-09-17"],
    "crew": 9,
    "status": 2,
    "place": {
      "sea": "Balearic Islands, Mediterranean Sea",
      "base": "Palma de Mallorca, Spain",
      "location": [2.64209867, 39.56770802]
    },
    "boat": {
      "name": "Unknown",
      "model": "Unknown",
      "build": 2018
    },
    "desc": "On the evening of the fourth day, we anchored off the Mirador de Na Clara in the Bay of Alcúdia amidst very strong, gusty winds. We maintained anchor watches in two-person teams, with each shift lasting two hours. The windlass gypsy could not withstand the force of the gusts, causing the chain to gradually pay out; I misinterpreted the sounds this process created. Finally, in the morning, the end of the chain slipped through and fell to the seabed. The anchor and chain were resting at a depth of about 4 to 5 meters. A crew member dove down and passed a line underneath the taut chain, allowing us to haul both the chain and the anchor back on board. As a lesson learned, I now always rig a bridle in strong winds to distribute the load to the bow cleats as well."
  },
